    "lawCode": "Amd §§250, 310, 410 & 1005, RWB L",
    "lawSection": "Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3023\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the racing, pari-mutuel wagering and breeding law, in\nrelation to the imposition of fines\n\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill increases from $5,000 to $25,000 the maximum amount of fine\nthat can be imposed for violation of the Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering\nand Breeding (Racing) Law.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends Racing Law . 250 (thoroughbred), which\nauthorizes imposition of monetary fines upon any corporation,\nassociation or person participating in racing (other than as a patron)\nat which pari-mutuel wagering is conducted, by increasing the maximum\nauthorization for fines from $5,000 to $25,000.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends Racing Law § 310 (harness), which deals\nwith fines.\n\nSection 3 of the bill amends Racing Law § 410 (quarterhorse), which\ndeals with fines.\n\nSection 4 of the bill amends Racing Law § 1005 (simulcasting), which\ndeals with fines.\n\nSection 5 of the bill contains the effective date.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nRacing Law §§ 250, 310, 410 and 1005 each contain a limitation of\n$5,000 per violation.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\n\nThe thoroughbred, harness, quarterhorse and simulcast articles of the\nRacing Law contain authorization for the imposition of fines up to\n$5,000 upon entities and individuals participating in pari-mutuel\nracing. The power to fine is the most practical enforcement method in\nracing and exists in all racing jurisdictions. Apart from\nsimulcasting, the $5,000 limitation has apparently been in place for\nmore than fifty years. It is no longer a practicable maximum amount.\n\nPeriodically, the $5,000 maximum fine has been inadequate to address\nthe severity of a violation--especially in relation to an entity that\nconducts horse racing or wagering. For such entities, the termination\nof operations or a portion thereof during a period of suspension of a\nlicense or plan of operation, with the resulting loss of revenues to\nall segments of the racing industry, as well as state and local\ngovernments, is a drastic action that could be taken in only the most\nserious instances. In order to enable the Board to properly discharge\nits oversight, supervisory and regulatory functions, the maximum\nauthorization for fines should be increased to $25,000.\n\n BUDGET IMPLICATIONS :\n\n\nThe fines provided for by this bill might raise a limited amount of\nrevenue for the State.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill shall take effect immediately.\n",
